# Revised: X_full=(local x, shared links); n_design_total includes both.
# ATC response targets use model-derived bounds; all block solvers share bounds.
# DON is online Adam optimization, not pretrained inference. Projected output
# allows exact box boundaries; clipping can still slow learning near saturation.
# CASE1B/1C vary both topology and total dimension (not isolated node effects).
# Q2/Q4/Q6/EXP are function families, not a guaranteed difficulty ranking.
# Reference errors are relative to a numerical SLSQP solution only.
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ATC-SQP vs ATC-DON controlled benchmark with target-response and linking variables.

Research goal
-------------
Compare SQP and a Deep Optimizer Network (DON) as local subproblem solvers
inside the *same* Analytical Target Cascading (ATC) coordination framework.

The benchmark deliberately uses a regular balanced B-ary tree and equal local
physical-design dimension m so that controlled case studies are possible.
It is not intended to reproduce every possible engineering ATC topology.

Each ATC element j has
----------------------
    x_j in R^m                         local physical design variables
    y_j in R                           local copy of a shared linking variable
                                       (non-root elements only)

The local analysis quantity is

    q_j(x_j, y_j)
      = sum_k w_k [x_jk + eta*x_jk^2] / sum_k w_k + beta_y*y_j,

    w_k = 1 + (k-1)/m.

The root has no incoming linking variable, so its y term is zero.

For response cascading,

    R_j = q_j                                             (leaf)
    R_j = alpha*q_j + (1-alpha)*mean(T_jc)               (internal)

where T_jc is the response target assigned by parent j to child c.  For every
edge e=(j,c), response consistency is

    c_e^R = T_jc - R_c = 0.

For linking-variable coordination, every internal parent j chooses one scalar
shared linking target Y_j for *all* of its children.  Each child c owns a local
copy y_c.  Hence the children of the same parent are coupled through

    c_e^Y = Y_j - y_c = 0,       e=(j,c).

This is a minimal, controlled realization of the ATC concept of a linking
variable shared among sibling elements.  The linking dimension is fixed at one
in the main study so it does not become another case-study factor.

The local physical inequality at every element remains

    g_j(x_j) = b - mean(x_j) <= 0,

with common box bounds on x, response targets, and linking variables.

Both ATC-SQP and ATC-DON use the same augmented-Lagrangian terms for response
consistency, linking consistency, and the same PHR inequality treatment.  The
principal difference is therefore the local subproblem solver.

The undecomposed/AIO reference contains one physical shared linking variable
Y_j for each internal parent j.  In the AIO model each child c uses the shared
value of its parent, y_c = Y_parent(c).  Child responses are recursively
substituted upward.  At simultaneous response and linking consistency, the
ATC decomposed objective equals the AIO objective.

Controlled factors
------------------
CASE 1A : local physical-design dimension m
CASE 1B : coordination breadth / branching factor B
CASE 1C : hierarchy depth D
CASE 1D : local objective nonlinearity kappa
CASE 1E : physical-constraint floor b (optional)
CASE 2  : DON hyperparameter sensitivity

Important scope limitation
--------------------------
The main benchmark fixes a regular balanced tree, a scalar linking variable,
equal local physical-design dimensions, and one common response/constraint
model.  Irregular trees, vector linking variables, heterogeneous dimensions,
and subsystem-specific physics are reserved for separate validation studies.
"""

